From: =?UTF-8?q?Eduardo=20Garc=C3=ADa-M=C3=A1dico=20Portabella?=
 <woodyst@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 12 Jul 2026 21:45:11 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 007/106] ASoC: tas2562: widen reset pulse settle time to 100ms

20ms/20ms (the vendor driver's own timing) caused the amp to fail
to probe at all when tested on-device - both codecs came up as
'dummy output' with no ALSA card at all. Manually re-triggering the
same probe path later (system fully booted, all rails/buses settled)
succeeded every time, pointing at an early-boot timing race rather
than a logic bug. Widen the settle time as a safe, conservative fix
since this early in boot the amp's supply rail may not have been
stable as long as it is by the time Android's userspace-triggered
HAL init runs.
---
 sound/soc/codecs/tas2562.c | 9 ++++++---
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sound/soc/codecs/tas2562.c b/sound/soc/codecs/tas2562.c
index 4f4d15392..e8c668fa5 100644
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/tas2562.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/tas2562.c
@@ -740,13 +740,16 @@ static int tas2562_probe(struct i2c_client *client)
 	 * (bootloader, a previous kernel boot, or a previous session) in
 	 * which case the amp would never actually go through power-on
 	 * reset and would keep whatever register state it already had.
-	 * Timing matches the vendor driver's own reset sequence.
+	 * This early in boot the amp's own supply rail may not have been
+	 * stable for long yet, so use a more generous settle time than
+	 * the vendor driver's own (userspace-triggered, well after boot)
+	 * 20ms/20ms sequence.
 	 */
 	if (data->sdz_gpio) {
 		gpiod_set_value_cansleep(data->sdz_gpio, 0);
-		msleep(20);
+		msleep(100);
 		gpiod_set_value_cansleep(data->sdz_gpio, 1);
-		msleep(20);
+		msleep(100);
 	}
 
 	data->regmap = devm_regmap_init_i2c(client, &tas2562_regmap_config);
